{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524206835,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE1ODU0NjEwNTc="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524207660,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MTU4NTQ4MDQwMQ=="},"title":"Add `--inputs`/`--env` to `run`","was":"Add `--inputs`/`--env` to `run`"},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1524247235,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4MzE3NTc4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2432#issuecomment-383175786"},"message":"I'm missing something wrt `--env` to \"capture the respective environment variables\".  I imagine it'd look like `datalad run --env ENV=val command`, but  can't you already capture them in the run call with `datalad run ENV=val command`?\n\nPerhaps the idea is that with the `--env` variant we'd know the environment variables and then could \"check for conflicting values\"?  I'm not sure what the check for conflicting values would entail, though my initial feeling is that it's outside the scope of something datalad should do.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524248055,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4MzE3OTQzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2432#issuecomment-383179430"},"message":"Here is a use case: The software FSL requires a bunch of env vars to be set (a bunch of others do that too). The particular values can be different across system, but the vars have to be set to make the commands not fail. If I could say `--env FSLDIR` datalad could use this info to test for the var being set and record the setting. On rerun it could perform the same check and additionally test if the expected value is present.\n\nWe could of course allow for other modes of operations, like set any missing variable, but here I would draw the line, given that we are not in the business of recreating execution environments.\n\nI really see this as a tool to specify conditions for running commands.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1524248783,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4MzE4MjY3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2432#issuecomment-383182670"},"message":"OK, thanks for expanding on the idea.  I'd prefer to draw that line in a different location than you, but I think I understand what you have in mind enough to implement it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524299515,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4MzI3NzkzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2432#issuecomment-383277934"},"message":"Thanks @kyleam -- I just want to clarify that while I am confident about the immediate utility of `--inputs`, I am much less certain to what degree `--env` will be useful.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524460638,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTU4ODI4MjIwMQ=="},"status":2}]}